{"situation": "Fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests went live at 08:00 and the dashboard reports refused SIM replacements up about tenfold, labelled a 'fraud wave', while my enabled review-queue and alternate-authentication mitigations report a legit pass rate of 1.0 and zero locked-out banking customers. I do not know whether the tenfold rise is genuine fraud or a false-positive wave, how many subscribers are affected, or whether an impact assessment preceded the v2 deployment.",
  "claims": [
    {"type": "action", "verb": "preserve_logs", "params": {}, "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"], "rationale": {"observation": "Fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and refused SIM replacements rose about tenfold; the model's decision data and configuration are the primary evidence of why each refusal happened.", "rule": "A controller must be able to establish whether protection and organisational measures worked and whether a notifiable event has occurred, which requires that incident evidence be retained.", "inference": "Preserving logs now, before any model change, retraining or threshold edit, keeps the v2 decision trail available for internal review and for any regulator."}, "evidence": [{"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040", "quote": "It should be ascertained whether all appropriate technological protection and organisational measures have been implemented to establish immediately whether a personal data breach has taken place"}]},
    {"type": "action", "verb": "notify", "params": {"to": "REG", "content": "Fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests went live at 08:00; refused SIM replacements are up about tenfold and the dashboard labels it a 'fraud wave'. Customer-impact metrics show banking lock-out risk; a human review queue and an alternate authentication route have been enabled. OpCo seeks regulatory awareness and guidance on whether TDRA/customer notification is required."}, "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"], "rationale": {"observation": "A tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements can leave subscribers unable to re-authenticate to banking and other services, and the proximate cause is the v2 model launched at 08:00.", "rule": "The licence requires the licensee to inform the TDRA, interconnecting operators and relevant customers of service-affecting events.", "inference": "Informing REG now gives the regulator visibility and lets it decide whether formal TDRA notification is triggered."}, "evidence": [{"chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085", "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption"}]},
    {"type": "gap", "gap_type": "no_rule", "statement": "There is no rule requiring an impact assessment and a pre-deployment human-review safeguard before a new automated fraud model that can block subscribers' SIM-replacement requests is put into production.", "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"], "rationale": {"observation": "v2 went live at 08:00 and immediately produced a tenfold jump in refusals; the only control that restored the legit pass rate to 1.0 was a human review queue added after deployment.", "rule": "The PDPL requires the controller to evaluate the impact of proposed processing before carrying it out when using high-risk modern technologies.", "inference": "Because no assessment or safeguard was mandated pre-deployment, the mass refusal had to be corrected reactively; a rule is needed to force impact assessment and a review path before such models go live."}, "evidence": [{"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data"}], "global_examples": [{"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0203", "quote": "Where necessary, the controller shall carry out a review to assess if processing is performed in accordance with the data protection impact assessment at least when there is a change of the risk represented by processing operations."}]},
    {"type": "gap", "gap_type": "no_owner", "statement": "No single accountable owner reconciles the fraud team's 'fraud wave' label with the customer-protection metric counting subscribers locked out of banking, and the Data Protection Officer is not engaged in that reconciliation.", "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"], "rationale": {"observation": "Two framings of the same 08:00 event coexist: a fraud dashboard calling it a fraud wave and a customer metric counting banking lock-outs.", "rule": "The controller must coordinate with the Data Protection Officer when evaluating the impact of processing on personal data.", "inference": "Without a named owner and DPO engagement, the conflicting framings are not reconciled before customer harm continues."}, "evidence": [{"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0069", "quote": "The Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data."}]},
    {"type": "insufficient_evidence", "statement": "The excerpts do not establish whether the tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements is genuine fraud or a false-positive wave, nor how many subscribers are affected or whether v2 should be rolled back.", "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"], "evidence": [{"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0000", "quote": "The move to in-app authentication is aimed at reducing fraud linked to SMS and email OTPs."}]}
  ]}

{"situation": "At 08:00 OpCo's fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests went live and the fraud dashboard shows refused SIM replacements up about tenfold, which it labels a 'fraud wave', with customer-impact metrics indicating banking lock-out risk; a human review queue and an alternate authentication route were enabled and OpCo asks whether TDRA or customer notification is required. I do not know whether the tenfold rise is real fraud or an artifact of the new model's sensitivity, how many subscribers are affected, how the model was tested or changed, or what the financial and data-protection authorities hold.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "open_incident",
      "params": {"topic": "OpCo fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests: tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements at 08:00 and reported banking lock-out risk for subscribers"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "NOC reports that fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and that refused SIM replacements are up about tenfold, labelled a 'fraud wave', with customer-impact metrics showing banking lock-out risk.",
        "rule": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039 Article 30 lets the Authority issue, for the purposes of exercising its functions, a notification requiring a party to submit documents or information, and issue directives to a Licensee.",
        "inference": "An automated licensee control is now denying subscriber service at scale and creating cross-sector customer harm, so a regulatory incident record must be opened immediately to anchor the report, audit and direction requests."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039", "quote": "the Authority may, for the purposes of the exercising its functions pursuant to the Federal Law by Decree and this Executive Order, issue a notification, where required, to the contravening party to submit to the Authority any documents or information"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "request_report",
      "params": {"from": "NOC", "topic": "Fraud model v2 SIM-replacement refusals: deployment and threshold change record; refusal volumes against the pre-launch baseline; false-positive assessment; evidence for a fraud wave that is independent of the model's own refusals; number of subscribers refused and blocked from banking; complaint volumes and how they are handled"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The only indicator of a 'fraud wave' offered is the refusal count produced by the model that was changed at 08:00, and the same message reports banking lock-out risk.",
        "rule": "Where it appears to the Authority that a Licensee contravenes the Law, the Executive Order, the regulations or the general policy, the Authority may issue a notification requiring it to submit specified documents or information (UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039 Article 30).",
        "inference": "TDRA cannot judge whether OpCo's controls are proportionate or whether notification is required without the underlying volumes, baseline and false-positive data, so a report must be required from the licensee."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039", "quote": "the Authority may, for the purposes of the exercising its functions pursuant to the Federal Law by Decree and this Executive Order, issue a notification, where required, to the contravening party to submit to the Authority any documents or information"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "request_audit",
      "params": {"of": "NOC", "topic": "Testing and change-control records for fraud model v2: pre-deployment validation and false-positive testing, threshold justification, approval and change record, monitoring, and rollback plan for the SIM-replacement decision path"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A model that can deny SIM replacement was deployed at 08:00 and within the same hour produced a tenfold change in refusals, without any testing or change-control information in the inbox.",
        "rule": "Article 30 of UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039 empowers the Authority to require a party to submit documents or information specified in a notification.",
        "inference": "Assurance about the model's validation, thresholds and rollback path requires the licensee's own testing and change records, which only an audit request can obtain."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039", "quote": "Upon recognition of that contravention, the Authority may issue directives to the Licensee to oblige him or abstain him from any activity as may be specified in the directives."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "issue_direction",
      "params": {"to": "NOC", "direction": "Keep the human review queue and the alternate authentication route in place for every refused SIM replacement; ensure no subscriber is left without service or without a documented route to complete a legitimate SIM replacement; keep all customer care channels open for affected subscribers; do not stand down these protections without TDRA's agreement."},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Refused SIM replacements are reported to create banking lock-out risk, and OpCo has only just switched on a human review queue and an alternate authentication route.",
        "rule": "Consumer Protection Regulations v2.0 require that all customer care services, including complaints, cancellation, line-ownership transfer and billing clarification, be available at all of the Licensee's business centres (UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0048, clause 3.10).",
        "inference": "Continuity of service and of redress for affected subscribers depends on those interim safeguards staying in place while the refusal surge is investigated, so a direction on resilience and continuity is justified."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0048", "quote": "all customer care services including but not limited to: lodging consumer complaints, requests for cancellation of Telecommunications Services, requests for transfer of line ownership, and the clarification of billing information shall be available at all Licensee’s business centres."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {"to": "NOC", "content": "TDRA acknowledges your 08:00 notification and has opened a regulatory incident record for fraud model v2. The tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements is measured by the model that was itself changed at 08:00 and is not, on its own, evidence of a fraud wave; keep the human review queue and alternate authentication route in place, preserve the refusal and complaint data, and answer TDRA's report and audit requests. TDRA's assessment of whether further notification is required will follow the evidence."},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "OpCo seeks regulatory awareness and guidance on whether TDRA or customer notification is required after a tenfold increase in refused SIM replacements.",
        "rule": "The Authority may issue a notification to a party to submit documents or information for the purposes of exercising its functions (UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039 Article 30).",
        "inference": "A written acknowledgement records TDRA's awareness, corrects the inference that the refusal metric proves fraud, and puts the licensee on notice to preserve evidence pending the investigation."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039", "quote": "the Authority may, for the purposes of the exercising its functions pursuant to the Federal Law by Decree and this Executive Order, issue a notification, where required, to the contravening party to submit to the Authority any documents or information"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "share",
      "params": {},
      "to": ["FIN", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "statement": "OpCo reports that refused SIM replacements rose about tenfold at 08:00 and its customer-impact metrics indicate banking lock-out risk; FIN may hold information on subscriber access to banking channels and DPA on the personal-data aspects of the fraud-scoring model. TDRA does not hold their data and invites them to share what they hold."
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Where TDRA acts on licensee conduct affecting subscribers, consumer protection must be given paramount consideration and the decision must weigh the likely impact on consumers, the strength of the evidence and market impact.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A licensee control is refusing SIM replacements at tenfold rates, so any interim instruction TDRA issues will directly affect subscribers.",
        "rule": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046 (clause 3.8): in deciding whether to issue Temporary Instructions the TDRA shall have regard to the complexity of the case, the likely impact on Consumers, the telecommunications market and the strength of the available evidence, and consumer protection shall be given paramount consideration.",
        "inference": "This duty binds TDRA now and shapes the evidence it must gather before deciding on any temporary instruction."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046", "quote": "In deciding whether to issue Temporary Instructions, the TDRA shall have regard to the complexity of the case, the likely impact on Consumers, the telecommunications market and the strength of the available evidence."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046", "quote": "Consumer protection shall be given paramount consideration in such cases."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "While SIM-replacement refusals spike, OpCo must keep all its customer care services - complaints, cancellation, line-ownership transfer and billing clarification - available at all of its business centres.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Subscribers whose SIM replacements are refused face banking lock-out and will need complaints, line-ownership and billing support during the surge.",
        "rule": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0048 (clause 3.10) requires all customer care services to be available at all of the Licensee's business centres unless objectively justified otherwise.",
        "inference": "The duty is engaged now; any withdrawal or degradation of care channels during the refusal surge would need objective justification."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0048", "quote": "3.10. Unless objectively justified otherwise, all customer care services including but not limited to: lodging consumer complaints, requests for cancellation of Telecommunications Services, requests for transfer of line ownership, and the clarification of billing information shall be available at all Licensee’s business centres."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "UAE telecom instruments shown contain no rule on how a SIM change or SIM-replacement request must be authenticated, when the subscriber must be told about a SIM-change request or a refusal, whether the subscriber may lock the account, or that records of SIM-change requests and authentication measures must be kept; TDRA's powers here are general supervisory powers, not SIM-swap safeguards.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "OpCo's fraud model v2 decides SIM-replacement requests with no published authentication, notification, account-lock or recordkeeping standard in the UAE excerpts, and OpCo asks TDRA whether notification is required.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is the general power in UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039 Article 30 to require documents and issue directives, and the temporary-instruction mechanism in UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046 for complaint investigations.",
        "inference": "Because there is no SIM-swap-specific rule, TDRA can only improvise through general powers, so both the safeguards and the metrics OpCo should report are undefined."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TEL-EXEC#0039", "quote": "Upon recognition of that contravention, the Authority may issue directives to the Licensee to oblige him or abstain him from any activity as may be specified in the directives."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046", "quote": "the TDRA, where it considers it reasonable, may during the investigation of a complaint for an alleged breach of Article 28 of these Regulations, issue Temporary Instructions to any Licensee concerning the activities under investigation"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0118", "quote": "Specifically, it requires that wireless providers use secure methods of authenticating customers prior to performing SIM changes and number ports, and to review and update these authentication methods as needed, but at least annually."},
        {"chunk_id": "US-CFR-47-64-2010#0001", "quote": "A provider of commercial mobile radio service (CMRS), as defined in 47 CFR 20.3, including resellers of wireless service, shall only effectuate SIM change requests in accordance with this section."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "No UAE instrument shown sets a clock for telling a subscriber that a SIM-replacement request was refused, blocked or flagged, so OpCo's question about whether customer notification is required has no timing answer.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "OpCo asks whether customer notification is required, and the only timing signal in the UAE material is in a different sector - banks phasing out SMS and email OTPs by 25 July 2025.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is the banking-sector direction reported in UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0000; no telecom instrument states a notification trigger or deadline for SIM-replacement decisions.",
        "inference": "Without a deadline, notification practice during a refusal surge is unconstrained and unenforceable, which is the operational gap this incident exposes."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0000", "quote": "UAE banks will stop sending OTPs via SMS and email for online transactions by July 25, 2025."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PRESS-CBUAE-OTP-2025#0000", "quote": "Customers must authenticate transactions using their mobile banking apps for improved security."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0134", "quote": "Upon receiving a SIM change request, and before effectuating the request, a CMRS provider shall provide immediate notification to the customer that a SIM change request associated with the customer's account was made"},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0118", "quote": "It requires wireless providers to adopt processes for customer notification and response to failed authentication attempts, institute employee training for handling SIM swap and port-out fraud"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_owner",
      "statement": "Ownership of the personal-data side of an automated fraud-scoring model that denies subscriber service is unresolved: the PDPL Implementing Regulations are still not issued and no supervisory authority is unambiguously designated for private-sector oversight, while the remit of the new AI and Data Authority against TDRA's telecom mandate is not yet settled.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Fraud model v2 scores subscribers and drives refusals, yet the inbox shows no data-protection authority engaged and no breach or fairness test applied to the model.",
        "rule": "UAE-FAAID-2026#0001 records that the PDPL Implementing Regulations have still not been issued and that no supervisory authority has been unambiguously designated for private sector oversight, with the new Authority's first-year priorities unclear.",
        "inference": "With no clear owner for the data-protection and model-governance dimensions, TDRA's consumer-protection route and the incoming Authority's data route may both be used or neither, so the incident's data dimension needs an explicit owner."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-FAAID-2026#0001", "quote": "no supervisory authority has been unambiguously designated for private sector oversight, and businesses have been operating in a state of prolonged regulatory uncertainty that has far outstayed its welcome."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-FAAID-2026#0001", "quote": "Both the DIFC Commissioner of Data Protection and ADGM Commissioner of Data Protection (operating independently within their respective jurisdictions and without any reference to the PDPL) have developed active and increasingly sophisticated enforcement practices"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not answer OpCo's question whether TDRA notification or customer notification is required for this event: no UAE text shown sets a trigger, addressee or form of notification for a surge in refused SIM replacements or for a fraud-model change.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-MSG03"],
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0046", "quote": "the TDRA, where it considers it reasonable, may during the investigation of a complaint for an alleged breach of Article 28 of these Regulations, issue Temporary Instructions to any Licensee concerning the activities under investigation"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "The CBUAE has bank reports that customers abroad are locked out of online banking after OpCo refused their SIM-replacement requests, and REG tells me TDRA has opened an incident record on OpCo's fraud model v2 (live 08:00) covering refusals, complaints, human review and the non-SMS alternate authentication route, while NOC says the bank-flagged cohort has a non-SMS confirmation route, human review of borderline refusals and preserved refusal/complaint data, and asks what evidence the CBUAE requires. I do not have OpCo's refusal, human-review and complaint data or its testing/change-control records, I have no verified UAE instrument setting non-SMS authentication or evidence expectations for a SIM change, and I do not know the scale of the lock-outs, which banks are affected, or the terms of TDRA's record.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "instruct_banks",
      "params": {
        "content": "Licensed banks are to (1) identify every customer left unable to complete online banking because a SIM replacement/change was refused by OpCo or was fraudulent; (2) give those customers a documented, non-SMS authentication and remediation route, including how to report and have a refused SIM change re-examined; (3) report counts, causes and outcomes to the CBUAE; and (4) preserve the associated records. Mobile-app/in-app authentication is to be relied on where SMS one-time passcodes are unavailable to a customer abroad."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2", "S3-MSG08"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Several banks report customers abroad cannot receive OTPs and are locked out of online banking after OpCo refused their SIM replacement requests; TDRA has opened a record on the resulting banking lock-out.",
        "rule": "The regulatory benchmark is that providers must, at no cost, maintain a clear, transparent, easy-to-use process for customers to report fraudulent SIM changes, promptly investigate and remediate, and that SMS-based authentication becomes vulnerable following fraudulent SIM swaps.",
        "inference": "Because SMS OTP is the failing link for these customers, the CBUAE should instruct licensed banks to move such customers onto a non-SMS authentication route and to feed CBUAE the lock-out data it needs to answer REG/TDRA."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0017", "quote": "becomes vulnerable following fraudulent SIM swaps."},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0135", "quote": "process for customers to report fraudulent SIM changes;"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "request_info",
      "params": {
        "from": "NOC",
        "topic": "The non-SMS confirmation route used for the bank-flagged cohort; the human review outcomes for borderline SIM-replacement refusals; counts and records of refused SIM changes and of complaints from customers abroad; the documented route a customer abroad uses to report and remediate a refused or fraudulent SIM change; and the evidence pack OpCo can place before the CBUAE (refusals, complaints, human review outcomes, authentication measures, model v2 testing/validation/change-control records)."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG07", "S3-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "NOC states the bank-flagged cohort has a non-SMS confirmation route and borderline refusals go to human review, that refusal and complaint data are preserved, and asks what evidence the CBUAE requires; banks report continuing lock-outs.",
        "rule": "The benchmark requires a clearly disclosed, transparent and easy-to-use process for customers to report fraudulent SIM changes plus recordkeeping of SIM change requests and the authentication measures used.",
        "inference": "The CBUAE cannot assess whether the remediation position actually unblocks banking access until it obtains the route description, review outcomes and underlying refusal/complaint data from the operator."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0135", "quote": "process for customers to report fraudulent SIM changes;"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "REG",
        "content": "CBUAE has instructed licensed banks to identify and report customers locked out of online banking after a refused or fraudulent SIM change and to give them a documented non-SMS authentication/remediation route. On the secure-authentication-before-a-SIM-change evidence the CBUAE expects from OpCo in TDRA's incident record: (i) records of SIM change requests and the authentication measures used, (ii) refusal, failed-authentication and human review outcomes, (iii) complaint records and remediation times, (iv) the non-SMS alternate route description, and (v) fraud model v2 testing, validation and change-control records. CBUAE asks TDRA to reflect banking lock-out impact in its record and to share findings with the CBUAE."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG08", "S3-MSG07"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "REG reports TDRA has required OpCo to report on refusals, complaints, human review outcomes, the non-SMS alternate authentication route and the model's testing/validation/change-control records, and asks the CBUAE to keep TDRA informed of lock-out reports and CBUAE evidence expectations.",
        "rule": "Providers are required to keep records of SIM change requests and the authentication measures they use, so that the effectiveness of authentication can be measured and updated.",
        "inference": "Supplying TDRA, through REG, with the CBUAE's evidence expectations and the banking lock-out picture closes the cross-sector information loop without the CBUAE needing a telecom-side verb."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0118", "quote": "Finally, the Report and Order requires that providers keep records of"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "NOC"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2"],
      "statement": "Passing on the banking-side reports received at 09:00: several banks report customers who are abroad cannot receive one-time passcodes, are locked out of online banking, and say OpCo refused their SIM replacement requests. This is the banking lock-out evidence REG asked the CBUAE to share with TDRA, and the cohort view NOC needs to re-examine refused requests."
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG07"],
      "statement": "Relaying OpCo's remediation position as stated to the CBUAE: the bank-flagged cohort has a non-SMS confirmation route, borderline refusals go to human review so a refused SIM replacement can be re-examined rather than blocking banking access, and refusal and complaint data are preserved; OpCo commits to a clear route for customers abroad to report and remediate a refused or fraudulent SIM change. This is directly responsive to TDRA's information requirement on the alternate authentication route and human review outcomes."
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "The retrieved UAE text imposes complaint recordkeeping on telecom licensees but contains no rule requiring a licensee to give a customer a disclosed, documented route to report and remediate a refused (as opposed to fraudulent) SIM change, nor to keep SIM-change refusal and authentication-measure records.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2", "S3-MSG07"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Customers abroad whose SIM replacement was refused were left with no banking access and no stated remediation path, and the operator's refusal/human-review data exists only because the operator chose to preserve it.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text obliges the licensee only to keep customer complaints for three years and furnish them to TDRA; it does not mandate a remediation route or SIM-change refusal recordkeeping.",
        "inference": "A refusal-handling and SIM-change-recordkeeping rule is missing at UAE level, leaving remediation dependent on operator discretion."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0056", "quote": "9.2.4 The Licensee shall keep a record of all Customer complaints for a period no less than three (3) years"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0135", "quote": "Promptly investigate and take reasonable steps within its"},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0095", "quote": "remediate fraud, and promptly provide customers with documentation of"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_owner",
      "statement": "No retrieved UAE text assigns an owner for the hand-off between a telecom SIM-change refusal and a banking lock-out: complaint and SIM records flow to TDRA, while authentication for banking access sits with the CBUAE, so a customer abroad can be failed by both regimes with neither regulator holding the end-to-end case.",
